1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,008
UOB: The United Chinese Bank of

2
00:00:03,088 --> 00:00:06,375
1935 and the Family That Built It

3
00:00:06,375 --> 00:00:11,151
On the morning of the 1st of
October 1935, a new bank opened its

4
00:00:11,231 --> 00:00:16,994
doors at the corner of Chulia Street
and Bonham Street, facing Raffles Place,

5
00:00:17,074 --> 00:00:21,475
in premises formerly occupied
by the Yokohama Specie Bank.

6
00:00:21,475 --> 00:00:25,729
It was called the United Chinese
Bank, it had seven founders and

7
00:00:25,809 --> 00:00:29,590
$1 million of paid-up capital,
and it was set up to serve

8
00:00:29,670 --> 00:00:34,275
Singapore's Hokkien merchants in
the middle of the Great Depression.

9
00:00:34,275 --> 00:00:37,737
Ninety years later, as
United Overseas Bank,

10
00:00:37,817 --> 00:00:41,600
it is one of Singapore's
three big local banks.

11
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:45,040
Much of that growth came
from buying other banks,

12
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:47,275
one of them older than itself.

13
00:00:47,275 --> 00:00:52,703
The United Chinese Bank was
incorporated on the 6th of August 1935

14
00:00:52,783 --> 00:00:58,125
by a group of seven Hokkien
businessmen led by Wee-Kheng-Chiang.

15
00:00:58,125 --> 00:01:03,080
Wee was born in Kuching,
Sarawak, in 1890, and had already

16
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:07,675
founded a bank there, the
Bian Chiang Bank, in 1924.

17
00:01:07,675 --> 00:01:12,175
He was the largest shareholder and
became the new bank's chairman.

18
00:01:12,175 --> 00:01:15,825
The Straits Times reported
the opening on the 1st of

19
00:01:15,905 --> 00:01:19,850
October under the headline
"Chinese Bank Opened Today."

20
00:01:19,850 --> 00:01:24,793
The bank had an authorised capital
of $4,000,000, of which $1,000,000

21
00:01:24,873 --> 00:01:29,088
had been issued, and it intended
to handle foreign exchange

22
00:01:29,168 --> 00:01:33,900
as well as ordinary banking,
current accounts and fixed deposits.

23
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:36,570
Ong-Piah-Teng was managing director,

24
00:01:36,650 --> 00:01:39,400
and there were five other directors.

25
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:43,681
The manager, Chua-Keh-Hai, was
a former municipal commissioner,

26
00:01:43,761 --> 00:01:47,142
and the paper described
him as the first banker to

27
00:01:47,222 --> 00:01:51,375
introduce foreign exchange
business among the local Chinese.

28
00:01:51,375 --> 00:01:54,644
The bank's own advertisement,
which ran in the

29
00:01:54,724 --> 00:01:57,921
Malaya Tribune the next
day, gave its address

30
00:01:58,001 --> 00:02:01,350
as 31-A Chulia Street,
in the Bonham Building.

31
00:02:01,350 --> 00:02:04,569
It was a single office,
and its customers were

32
00:02:04,649 --> 00:02:08,450
mainly Hokkien traders
looking for short-term credit.

33
00:02:08,450 --> 00:02:11,050
It joined a crowded field.

34
00:02:11,050 --> 00:02:14,533
Singapore already had a
number of Chinese banks,

35
00:02:14,613 --> 00:02:19,061
each tied to a dialect group,
and three of them had merged in

36
00:02:19,141 --> 00:02:23,075
1932 to form the Oversea-Chinese
Banking Corporation.

37
00:02:23,075 --> 00:02:25,836
Little has been published about how the

38
00:02:25,916 --> 00:02:28,975
bank fared during the Japanese Occupation.

39
00:02:28,975 --> 00:02:32,229
Wee-Kheng-Chiang's
fourth son, Wee-Cho-Yaw,

40
00:02:32,309 --> 00:02:35,873
born in 1929, spent most
of the Occupation with

41
00:02:35,953 --> 00:02:39,675
his family in Karimun,
in what is now Indonesia.

42
00:02:39,675 --> 00:02:45,075
In 1958 he joined the bank's
board as its youngest director.

43
00:02:45,075 --> 00:02:48,970
On the 1st of July 1960,
when his father stepped down

44
00:02:49,050 --> 00:02:53,025
as managing director,
Wee-Cho-Yaw took over the post.

45
00:02:53,025 --> 00:02:55,100
He was 31.

46
00:02:55,100 --> 00:02:58,333
In the late 1950s and early 1960s

47
00:02:58,413 --> 00:03:01,325
the bank grew in small steps.

48
00:03:01,325 --> 00:03:06,634
It drove its first mobile branch
in 1958, opened its first branch

49
00:03:06,714 --> 00:03:11,692
outside the head office, on
Beach Road, in 1959, and in 1962,

50
00:03:11,772 --> 00:03:17,825
by its own account, became the first
bank to focus on women as customers.

51
00:03:17,825 --> 00:03:21,581
On the 23rd of January 1965 the United

52
00:03:21,661 --> 00:03:25,800
Chinese Bank became United Overseas Bank.

53
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:29,302
The change avoided confusion
with another United

54
00:03:29,382 --> 00:03:32,362
Chinese Bank in Hong Kong, where the bank

55
00:03:32,442 --> 00:03:35,950
opened its first overseas
branch the same year.

56
00:03:35,950 --> 00:03:40,525
From 1971 the bank grew mainly
by acquiring other banks,

57
00:03:40,605 --> 00:03:45,675
each an independent bank with
its own founders and customers.

58
00:03:45,675 --> 00:03:49,332
The first was Chung Khiaw
Bank, founded by the

59
00:03:49,413 --> 00:03:53,475
Tiger Balm magnate Aw-Boon-Haw
and opened in 1950.

60
00:03:53,475 --> 00:03:58,509
UOB bought a majority stake in
it in 1971, after the Aw family's

61
00:03:58,589 --> 00:04:04,341
holding company had passed to the
British investment group Slater Walker,

62
00:04:04,421 --> 00:04:09,375
and Chung Khiaw went on trading
under its own name until 1999.

63
00:04:09,375 --> 00:04:12,887
In 1973 UOB acquired Lee Wah Bank,

64
00:04:12,967 --> 00:04:19,200
a Cantonese bank founded in
1920 and older than UOB itself.

65
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:23,739
It took a majority interest
in Far Eastern Bank,

66
00:04:23,819 --> 00:04:28,261
founded in 1958, in 1984,
and in the Industrial

67
00:04:28,341 --> 00:04:32,575
& Commercial Bank,
founded in 1953, in 1987.

68
00:04:32,575 --> 00:04:36,622
In 1974 Wee-Kheng-Chiang
retired and Wee-Cho-Yaw

69
00:04:36,702 --> 00:04:40,400
became chairman as well
as chief executive.

70
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:44,699
The same year the bank moved into
a new 30-storey building on the

71
00:04:44,779 --> 00:04:49,225
site of the Bonham Building, where
it had rented its first office.

72
00:04:49,225 --> 00:04:51,687
The taller UOB Plaza One,

73
00:04:51,767 --> 00:04:55,225
on the same block, opened in 1995.

74
00:04:55,225 --> 00:04:58,925
The largest purchase came in 2001.

75
00:04:58,925 --> 00:05:03,994
Overseas Union Bank had been
founded by Lien-Ying-Chow and opened

76
00:05:04,074 --> 00:05:09,459
on the 5th of February 1949, the
first new bank in postwar Singapore,

77
00:05:09,539 --> 00:05:13,975
and by the 1970s it was one
of the four big local banks.

78
00:05:13,975 --> 00:05:19,012
In June 2001, when local banks
were under pressure to consolidate,

79
00:05:19,092 --> 00:05:22,113
the Development Bank of Singapore made a

80
00:05:22,193 --> 00:05:26,225
hostile bid of 9.4 billion
Singapore dollars for it.

81
00:05:26,225 --> 00:05:30,456
UOB answered with a bid of
10 billion Singapore dollars

82
00:05:30,536 --> 00:05:34,925
that had the support of OUB's
board and of Lien himself.

83
00:05:34,925 --> 00:05:38,662
In September 2001 UOB
announced that it had won

84
00:05:38,742 --> 00:05:43,048
acceptances for more than
90 per cent of OUB's shares,

85
00:05:43,128 --> 00:05:47,678
and the deal made it the
largest local bank in Singapore,

86
00:05:47,758 --> 00:05:51,900
with about 115 billion
Singapore dollars in assets.

87
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:56,425
The OUB name was gone by the end of 2003.

88
00:05:56,425 --> 00:06:00,234
Wee-Cho-Yaw stepped down
as chief executive in 2007,

89
00:06:00,314 --> 00:06:05,550
handing the post to his eldest son,
Wee-Ee-Cheong, who still holds it.

90
00:06:05,550 --> 00:06:09,037
He retired from the board in 2018

91
00:06:09,117 --> 00:06:12,900
and died in February 2024, aged 95.

92
00:06:12,900 --> 00:06:17,457
The chart shows the five banks
that UOB acquired in Singapore,

93
00:06:17,537 --> 00:06:22,100
from the year each opened to
the year UOB took control of it.

94
00:06:22,100 --> 00:06:25,829
In 1935 the United Chinese
Bank was one of many

95
00:06:25,909 --> 00:06:30,205
small Chinese banks in
Singapore, most of them serving

96
00:06:30,285 --> 00:06:34,175
a single dialect community
from a single office.

97
00:06:34,175 --> 00:06:39,317
Today Singapore has three large
local banks, UOB, OCBC and DBS,

98
00:06:39,397 --> 00:06:45,200
and several of the banks of that
earlier era survive only inside them.

99
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:49,330
Mergers and acquisitions
did that over seven decades,

100
00:06:49,410 --> 00:06:54,175
and the last big round, in 2001,
left the three that remain.

101
00:06:54,175 --> 00:06:58,791
Where it fits in the bigger story:
UOB is one of the most familiar

102
00:06:58,871 --> 00:07:03,274
names in Singapore, but its
origin, a single rented office in a

103
00:07:03,354 --> 00:07:08,050
Depression-era building run for
Hokkien merchants, is rarely told.

104
00:07:08,050 --> 00:07:12,647
Its history is also a history of
the smaller banks it absorbed,

105
00:07:12,727 --> 00:07:17,250
from a Cantonese bank of 1920
to the Tiger Balm family's bank,

106
00:07:17,330 --> 00:07:20,300
whose names have largely been forgotten.
